  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1101 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\n00Z upper-air analysis places an upper-level low over New England\nwith a longwave trough extending south into the northern Gulf.\nAnother upper-level low is located over southern California with\nHurricane Polo located near Baja California. In between these two\nfeatures, an elongated upper-level ridge extends from the\nSouthern Plains northeast into the Western Great Lakes Region.\nSurface high pressure centered near southern Lake Michigan is\nbringing another dry and very pleasant evening across the Mid-\nSouth with late evening temperatures in the 60s.\n\nThe aforementioned surface high will bring another day of dry\nweather to the Mid-South with afternoon highs in the 80s to lower\n90s. NBM dewpoints continue to be too high in this dry pattern\nand have blended Tuesday afternoon values towards the NBM 10th\npercentile. This leads to high confidence in minimum relative\nhumidity values ranging again between 20 to 25% Tuesday afternoon\nwith an elevated fire danger persisting across the Mid-South.\n\nLatest mid to long term model trends indicate moisture gradually\nincreasing across the Lower Mississippi Valley towards late this\nweek as surface and upper-level ridging begin to move east and\nthe upper-level low and associated remnants of Hurricane Polo\nmove into the Southern Plains. The onset of rain chances have\ncontinued to be delayed in each subsequent model run with the\nlatest data supporting development late Thursday night into\nFriday as a weak stationary  boundary drops into the region. The\nhigher probabilities for rain showers will come this weekend as a\nnorthern stream trough moves across the Mississippi Valley and\nprecipitable water values approach 2 inches. Overall shear will\nremain weak thus best coverage will occur during peak heating.\nCooler air is expected to filter in behind the front towards the\nend of the upcoming weekend with highs in the 70s and lows in the\n50s to lower 60s.",
